Use two local models, and keep the contraindication decision in the formulary. A local model can rewrite a sentence and explain flags. It should not be the thing that decides whether Diclofenac is unsafe in deep-vein thrombosis.

Run them with Ollama on this machine. Download once while the network is up (`ollama pull …`). After that, inference stays on the Docker network.

**Custom wording.** Use **Qwen3 4B** (`ollama pull qwen3:4b`). This job is one JSON sentence, the same call as `override_reason` today. It needs about 6 GB of video memory, or it will run on CPU in a few seconds. **MedGemma 4B** (`ollama pull medgemma:4b`) is the alternative when you want the sentence in a clinical register. Either model is enough. The four preset reasons stay as fixed text and do not call a model.

**Short safety summary.** Use **MedGemma 4B** for the paragraph, with **Qwen3 8B** if you would rather keep a single model for both the sentence and the summary. Feed it only the anonymized context PHP already builds: age band, sex, conditions, findings, and the formulary flag rows. The summary then explains those rows. On a 24 GB card, **MedGemma 27B** writes a clearer paragraph, at roughly 16 GB for a 4-bit copy.

**Full consult with tool calls.** Use **Qwen3 14B**, or **Qwen3 30B-A3B** if you have 24 GB (`ollama pull qwen3:14b` or `qwen3:30b`). Qwen3 speaks Ollama’s tools API, so it can call `get_lab_tests_for_condition`, `get_cost_effective_tx`, and the ICD-11 lookup the way Gemini does now. **MedGemma does not.** Ollama rejects tool requests to it, so it cannot be the consult orchestrator.

| Machine | Wording | Safety summary | Consult |
|---|---|---|---|
| CPU, 16 GB RAM | Qwen3 4B | MedGemma 4B, slow | Catalog only |
| 8–12 GB GPU | Qwen3 4B | Qwen3 8B or MedGemma 4B | Qwen3 8B, short tool list |
| 16 GB GPU | Qwen3 4B | MedGemma 4B | Qwen3 14B |
| 24 GB GPU | Qwen3 4B | MedGemma 27B, loaded when needed | Qwen3 30B-A3B |

Load one large model at a time. Qwen3 30B-A3B and MedGemma 27B together need about 35 GB.

The reliable contraindication check stays in `DrugSafety` and the three formulary tables. Qwen3 retrieves labs and medicines. MedGemma writes the summary from those tool results plus the formulary flags. An empty `drug_contraindications` row still produces no flag, whichever local model you run.
